---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Setups — wAIbi-sabi"
  description="How I build things with Claude — starting with this site itself: a Notion-controlled content pipeline, two scheduled Claude Code agents, and a static Astro front end. Documented so it can be replicated."
  section="Setups"
  keywords={[
    'building with Claude Code',
    'Notion content pipeline',
    'Claude Code scheduled tasks',
    'Astro blog automation',
  ]}
>
  <section class="hero fade-up d1" style="padding-bottom:2rem">
    <span class="hanko">Setups</span>
    <h1>Things I built <span class="hl hl--plum">with Claude</span></h1>
    <p>
      Each entry is a working system, not a demo — what it does, how the pieces fit, and
      enough detail to rebuild it. Starting with the one you are reading.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="rulebar fade-up d2" aria-hidden="true">
    <span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span>
  </div>

  <article class="proj scroll-reveal" style="--accent:var(--mizu);--tint:var(--wash-mizu)">
    <header class="proj__head">
      <span class="proj__tag">01 · Live</span>
      <h2>This site — a self-writing blog</h2>
      <p class="proj__lede">
        A Notion-controlled content pipeline feeding a static Astro site. Two scheduled
        Claude Code agents do the research and drafting; I stay in the loop only where
        judgement is actually needed.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="body">
      <h3>What it does</h3>
      <p>
        Every morning an agent searches for what is moving in AI, checks it against
        everything already covered, researches the two or three strongest angles, and writes
        each one up as a 100-word pitch in a Notion database. I read the pitches, give each a
        rating and any remarks. Anything I rate 4 or 5 gets picked up within four hours by a
        second agent, which researches it properly and writes the full article. Drafts land
        back in Notion for review. Nothing publishes itself.
      </p>

      <h3>The pieces</h3>
      <div class="stack">
        <div class="layer">
          <span class="layer__k">Control plane</span>
          <p>
            Two Notion databases. <strong>AI Blog OS Pipeline</strong> holds radar ideas —
            title, 100-word pitch, SEO keywords, and the two fields that are mine alone:
            Interest Rating and Remarks. <strong>Article Production</strong> holds anything
            promoted to a full draft, related back to its source idea.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="layer">
          <span class="layer__k">Agents</span>
          <p>
            Two Claude Code scheduled tasks. <code>daily-ai-seo-radar</code> runs at 7:10am;
            <code>ai-article-writer</code> runs every four hours and processes at most two
            articles per run. Each task is a self-contained prompt — a fresh run has no
            memory of the conversation that created it, so every instruction it needs lives
            in the task itself.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="layer">
          <span class="layer__k">Skills</span>
          <p>
            <code>seo-topic-research</code> governs the research protocol and forbids
            inventing search volumes or rankings. <code>viral-hooks</code> works the opening,
            <code>storytelling</code> the through-line, and <code>anti-ai-writing</code> runs
            as a mandatory final filter on every draft.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="layer">
          <span class="layer__k">Front end</span>
          <p>
            Astro 5, static output, no client framework. Content collections validate every
            article's frontmatter against a schema — which has already caught a real bug, an
            over-length meta description that would have been truncated in search results.
            Sitemap, RSS, canonical URLs and JSON-LD are generated at build time.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="layer">
          <span class="layer__k">Archive</span>
          <p>
            The interview news archive is a third Notion database, pulled to local JSON by a
            sync script and rendered as filterable cards. The site builds offline; Notion is
            the source of truth, not a runtime dependency.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>What I would tell you before you copy it</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Put the human gate where judgement lives.</strong> The rating field is the
          whole design. Research and drafting are cheap to automate; deciding what is worth
          saying is not.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Write the guardrails into the task prompt.</strong> Mine forbid fabricated
          metrics, require a real source for every time-sensitive claim, and explicitly allow
          the agent to produce nothing on a quiet day. That last one matters more than it
          sounds — without it, an agent will pad.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Let the schema be strict.</strong> Validation is the only thing standing
          between an unattended pipeline and quiet degradation.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Watch the token cost of your setup.</strong> Skill and agent descriptions
          are injected into every session and every sub-agent, so a large library multiplies
          across a parallel run. Mine is deliberately trimmed, with the rest archived.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p class="proj__note">
        <a href="/blog/how-this-blog-builds-itself/">The full walkthrough is here</a>, with four
        architecture diagrams. They were derived from a NotebookLM notebook built on the actual
        configuration files — both scheduled-task prompts, the skills, the build config and all
        three Notion schemas — so they describe what the system does rather than what I remember
        it doing.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>

  {/* The two shorter entries sit side by side. They carry no long prose, so
      holding them to the reading measure only wasted the width. */}
  <div class="proj__pair">
    <article class="proj proj--compact scroll-reveal" style="--accent:var(--hanko);--tint:var(--wash-hanko)">
      <header class="proj__head">
        <span class="proj__tag">02 · Ongoing</span>
        <h2>Red teaming frontier models</h2>
        <p class="proj__lede">
          36 unique breaks on the Gray Swan Arena, mostly prompt injection and safeguard
          evasion. Writing up the transferable patterns — the ones that are about system
          design rather than any single model.
        </p>
      </header>
    </article>

    <article
      class="proj proj--compact scroll-reveal"
      style="--accent:var(--ochre);--tint:var(--wash-ochre);transition-delay:.09s"
    >
      <header class="proj__head">
        <span class="proj__tag">03 · Live</span>
        <h2>Interview News Archive</h2>
        <p class="proj__lede">
          A daily business-news digest for Indian B-school placement prep — 221 entries, each
          paired with the angle an interviewer would actually push on.
        </p>
        <a class="proj__cta" href="/news/">
          Browse it
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5"
            stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
            <path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6" />
          </svg>
        </a>
      </header>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  /* Matches the index: the palette stated once, drawing itself in. This bar
     was static here and animated there, which read as two different sites. */
  .rulebar { display: flex; gap: 3px; margin: 2.5rem 0 3rem; }
  .rulebar span {
    height: 3px; flex: 1; border-radius: 2px;
    transform-origin: left; animation: stretch .8s var(--ease) both;
  }
  .rulebar span:nth-child(1) { background: var(--mizu); animation-delay: .15s; }
  .rulebar span:nth-child(2) { background: var(--ochre); animation-delay: .22s; }
  .rulebar span:nth-child(3) { background: var(--moss); animation-delay: .29s; }
  .rulebar span:nth-child(4) { background: var(--plum); animation-delay: .36s; }
  .rulebar span:nth-child(5) { background: var(--hanko); animation-delay: .43s; }
  .rulebar span:nth-child(6) { background: var(--indigo); animation-delay: .5s; }
  @keyframes stretch { from { transform: scaleX(0); } to { transform: scaleX(1); } }

  .proj {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
    border-radius: 18px;
    background: var(--tint);
    padding: 1.75rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
    /* The left rule thickens and the card lifts — the same language the index
       track panels use, so an entry reads as reachable rather than inert. */
    transition: transform var(--dur-fast) var(--ease),
                border-left-width var(--dur-fast) var(--ease),
                border-color var(--dur-fast) var(--ease);
  }
  .proj:hover, .proj:focus-within {
    transform: translateY(-3px);
    border-left-width: 5px;
    border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 40%, var(--border));
  }
  @media (min-width: 768px) { .proj { padding: 2.25rem 2.5rem; } }

  /* Side-by-side for the entries that are a lede and nothing more. */
  .proj__pair { display: grid; gap: 1.25rem; align-items: start; }
  @media (min-width: 60rem) { .proj__pair { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; } }
  .proj--compact { max-width: none; margin-bottom: 0; height: 100%; }

  .proj__tag {
    font-size: .625rem; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: .15em;
    text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--accent);
  }
  .proj__head h2 {
    font-size: clamp(1.4rem, 3.5vw, 1.9rem); font-weight: 800; line-height: 1.15;
    letter-spacing: -.02em; margin: .6rem 0 .75rem; text-wrap: balance;
  }
  .proj__lede { font-size: 1rem; line-height: 1.65; color: var(--ink-soft); margin: 0; }

  .proj .body { margin-top: 2rem; }
  .proj .body h3 {
    font-size: .6875rem; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: .15em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--accent); margin: 2.25rem 0 .9rem;
  }
  .proj .body > p:first-of-type { font-size: 1rem; font-weight: 400; margin-bottom: 1.25rem; }

  .stack { display: grid; gap: .6rem; }
  .layer {
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--paper) 60%, transparent);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-soft);
    border-radius: 12px; padding: .9rem 1.1rem;
  }
  .layer__k {
    display: block; font-size: .5625rem; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: .16em;
    text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--accent); margin-bottom: .35rem;
  }
  .layer p { margin: 0; font-size: .875rem; line-height: 1.6; }

  .proj__note {
    margin-top: 2rem; padding: .9rem 1.1rem; border-radius: 12px;
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--paper) 60%, transparent);
    border: 1px dashed var(--border);
    font-size: .8125rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .proj__cta {
    display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: .45rem; margin-top: 1.1rem;
    font-size: .625rem; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: .12em; text-transform: uppercase;
    text-decoration: none; color: var(--paper); background: var(--ink);
    padding: .6rem 1rem; border-radius: 999px;
    transition: transform var(--dur-fast) var(--ease);
  }
  .proj__cta svg { width: 12px; height: 12px; transition: transform var(--dur-fast) var(--ease); }
  .proj__cta:hover { transform: translateY(-2px); }
  .proj__cta:hover svg { transform: translateX(3px); }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .rulebar span { animation: none; transform: none; }
    .proj:hover, .proj:focus-within, .proj__cta:hover { transform: none; }
  }
</style>
